{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given number.\n    Handles negative inputs by returning 0 if it's the next perfect square.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        # The smallest perfect square is 0. If n is negative, 0 is the smallest\n        # perfect square strictly greater than n.\n        return 0\n    else:\n        # For non-negative n, find its integer square root.\n        # The next perfect square will be the square of (its integer square root + 1).\n        s = math.isqrt(n)\n        return (s + 1) ** 2"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic subsequence is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(numbers)\n\n    # Calculate LIS (Longest Increasing Subsequence) sum ending at each index\n    lis_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis_sums[i] = numbers[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if numbers[j] < numbers[i]:\n                lis_sums[i] = max(lis_sums[i], lis_sums[j] + numbers[i])\n\n    # Calculate LDS (Longest Decreasing Subsequence) sum starting at each index\n    lds_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds_sums[i] = numbers[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if numbers[j] < numbers[i]:\n                lds_sums[i] = max(lds_sums[i], lds_sums[j] + numbers[i])\n\n    # Combine LIS and LDS sums to find the maximum bitonic subsequence sum.\n    # The peak element numbers[i] is counted in both LIS and LDS sums, so subtract it once.\n    return max(lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - numbers[i] for i in range(n))"}
